<article class="env-lemma" id="0GEC">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0GEC">Lemma <span data-tag="0GEC">10.63.20</span>.</a> Let $k$ be a field. Let $S$ be a finite type $k$ algebra. If $\dim (S) > 0$, then there exists an element $f \in S$ which is a nonzerodivisor and a nonunit. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Lemma <a href="/tag/00LC" data-tag="00LC">10.63.5</a> the ring $S$ has finitely many associated prime ideals. By Lemma <a href="/tag/0ALW" data-tag="0ALW">10.61.3</a> the ring $S$ has infinitely many maximal ideals. Hence we can choose a maximal ideal $\mathfrak m \subset S$ which is not an associated prime of $S$. By prime avoidance (Lemma <a href="/tag/00DS" data-tag="00DS">10.15.2</a>), we can choose a nonzero $f \in \mathfrak m$ which is not contained in any of the associated primes of $S$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/00LD" data-tag="00LD">10.63.9</a> the element $f$ is a nonzerodivisor and as $f \in \mathfrak m$ we see that $f$ is not a unit. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>